{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789966926,"nonce":"2LRYLkKO5fRx39Ko/vm36O10Aa0=","title":"The release facts of the linked library are rebuilt in every process, on the critical path of the first interpreted body","message":"`drop_insert::facts_of` runs over the whole module at start-up on the warm-up thread (`Facts::of` in `install_lazy_compiler`), and the main thread's first `make_interp_body` (the boxed-constant initializer) waits on it under the `Facts` mutex. Measured with `ZYNTAX_TRACE_DROP_TIME=1 ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1` on `print(1+2)` (Mac arm64, release, LLVM on):\n\n    zylua: returns_param 3.2 ms in 3 rounds over 301 functions, returns_owned 6.9 ms in 4 rounds; facts 10.4 ms; the main thread's `[INTERP] body of __zyntax_box_constants` reads 9.3 ms, all of it the wait\n    zypy:  returns_param 1.0 ms over 111 functions, returns_owned 1.6 ms; facts 2.7 ms\n\nThat is the largest single item left in zylua's 35 ms trivial run (26 ms inside main). The work is the same every process: nearly all of the module is the snapshot's library, whose facts (`returns_owned`, `returns_param`, `borrowed_params`, `string_makers`, `glue`) do not depend on the program. Store them in the snapshot beside the lowered module (ids relocated by the same base as the shell's), seed `ModuleFacts::build` with them, and run the two fixed points over the program's own functions only, with the library's answers as the starting facts of their callees.\n\nRelated: the adopted library bodies are also decoded from postcard in every process (`LazyModule::function`): 298 functions, 2.3 ms on zylua, 102 functions, 0.85 ms on zypy, the bulk of the `bodies` lap of `[LOWER-PROGRAM]`. A zero-copy body format or a decoded-body cache keyed by snapshot would remove that too.","files":null}]}